Suppose the Hessian matrix of $f(x, y)$ at a stationary point $bold(x_0) = (x_0, y_0)$ has non-zero eigenvalues, so that in coordinates $(xi, eta)$ aligned with its principal axes
$
  f(bold(x)) approx f(bold(x_0)) + (1/2) (lambda_1 xi^2 + lambda_2 eta^2).
$
On contours near $bold(x_0)$, where $f$ is constant,
$
  lambda_1 xi^2 + lambda_2 eta^2 = "constant".
$

- At a maximum or minimum, $lambda_1$ and $lambda_2$ have the same sign, and the contours are ellipses.

- At a saddle point, $lambda_1$ and $lambda_2$ have opposite signs, and the contours are hyperbolae.

In particular, a Hessian that is positive definite at $bold(x_0)$ makes $bold(x_0)$ a local minimum, a negative definite one a local maximum, while an indefinite Hessian may give a maximum, minimum or saddle point.
